    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0754" n="626" sameAs="frus:frus1928v01/pg626"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.1012 Washington/49</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gade</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-05-10"><hi rendition="#italic">May 10,
                        1928</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received June 8.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 620</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In compliance with the Department’s
                    instruction No. 129 of April 14, 1928,<anchor n="77" xml:id="d375-f1"/> I hava
                    the honor to report that I today presented to the Minister for Foreign Affairs a
                    note concerning the proposed Conference of Arbitration and Conciliation to be
                    held at Washington on December 10, 1928.</p>
                <p>In a conversation with the Chief of Protocol (<hi rendition="#italic">Director de
                        Secciones e Introductor de Embajadores</hi>) before my interview with the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs, Sr. Yeregui volunteered the information that the
                    Department’s proposals would surely receive a hearty acceptance as Uruguay was
                    unreservedly in favor of arbitration. He further declared that Uruguay had up to
                    now refused to conclude arbitration treaties which contained reservations, even
                    on matters of sovereignty.</p>
                <p>The Minister for Foreign Affairs, Sr. Dominguez, upon receiving the note stated
                    that Uruguay was in favor of the principle of arbitration and that an early
                    answer might be expected.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerhard Gade</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
